<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Histadrut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as General Federation of Labour</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Histadrut, the General Federation of Labour, was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> in 1920 as the trade union of Jewish workers in Mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. Under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ben-gurion">David Ben-Gurion</a> it also ran its own enterprises, including the construction firm Solel Boneh, a bank and a health fund, and became a pillar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yishuv">Yishuv</a>. It remains Israel's largest trade union federation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Histadrut was founded in December 1920 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> to look after the interests of Jewish workers. Until then the parties Ahdut HaAvoda and Hapoel Hatzair had been unable to set up a single workers' organisation. Membership grew from 4,400 in 1920 to 8,394 in 1922. By 1927 it had around 22,000 members, 68% of the Jewish workforce, and by 1939 about 100,000, some 75%.</p><p>In its first year it lacked central leadership and many initiatives were local. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ben-gurion">David Ben-Gurion</a> was elected Secretary at the end of 1921. He set up a defined hierarchy, reduced the powers of the local workers' councils and centralised the collection of dues, and he wanted to turn the federation into a national instrument for realising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a>. Its aims covered settlement, defence, trade unions, education, housing, health, banking, cooperative ventures, welfare and culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Absorbing immigrants was seen as a central task, sometimes ranked above financial soundness. The Histadrut's Office for Public Works went bankrupt in 1924 and its successor Solel Boneh in 1927, and the Zionist Executive bailed out both, entering the deficits as expenses for immigration absorption. By 1930 the Histadrut was the central organisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yishuv">Yishuv</a>. According to Tzahor it had become the executive arm of the Zionist movement, but an arm acting on its own, a state in the making. Ze'ev Sternhell argues instead that its leaders had already abandoned socialist principles by 1920 and used them only as mobilising myths.</p><p>Through its economic arm, Hevrat HaOvdim, it owned the country's largest industrial conglomerates, its largest bank, Bank Hapoalim, and its largest shipping company, ZIM. It also owned Clalit Health Services, which accepted members only if they belonged to the union, and in the mid-1980s an estimated 70% of Israelis were insured by Clalit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>From the 1980s liberalisation of the economy reduced the Histadrut's role. Hyperinflation left its business empire with large debts, and it lost control of Bank Hapoalim after the 1983 bank stock crisis, when the government nationalised the major banks. In 1994 the Labor Party lost its leadership of the Histadrut, and a new party, RAM, took charge and began to sell off its assets that were not tied to the union.</p><p>The National Health Insurance Law, in effect from 1995, severed Clalit's tie to the Histadrut. Membership fell from about 1.8 million, almost 80% of the workforce, to about 200,000. The Histadrut sold real estate to survive, then recovered gradually, and in 2005 it had about 650,000 members.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Histadrut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jewish institutions built under it carried over into a state: the Hebrew school system of 1919, the <b>Histadrut</b> of 1920, the Technion in 1924 and the Hebrew University of Jerusalem in 1925; the Haganah, the Palmach formed with British help in 1942 and veterans of the Jewish Brigade…</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>David Ben-Gurion, secretary general of the <b>Histadrut</b> and leader of the Zionist labour movement, declared the State of Israel; Labor Zionism was also shaped by Nachman Syrkin, Berl Katznelson, Ber Borochov and A. D. Gordon.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · People</a></li></ul></section>
